-- Update Beginner course template with full 42-day lesson content
UPDATE public.course_templates 
SET 
  duration_days = 42,
  peptides = '[
    {
      "name": "BPC-157",
      "purpose": "Body Protection Compound - accelerates healing throughout the body including tendons, ligaments, muscles, and gut",
      "dosing_research": "250mcg once daily for the entire course",
      "timing": "Any time of day - morning or evening, consistency matters more than timing",
      "frequency": "Once daily",
      "site": "Subcutaneous injection near injury site or abdomen for general benefits",
      "notes": "Single peptide, single injection per day. Perfect for beginners learning the basics."
    }
  ]'::jsonb,
  lessons = '[
    {"day": 0, "phase": "Getting Started", "title": "Welcome to Your First Peptide", "content": "Welcome to the Beginner Course—your introduction to peptides.\n\nYou''ve chosen the simplest, most beginner-friendly protocol available: one peptide, one injection per day. No complex stacking, no multiple daily doses, just a straightforward path to experiencing what peptides can do.\n\nWhy BPC-157 for beginners:\nBPC-157 (Body Protection Compound) is the ideal first peptide because:\n- It has an excellent safety profile with minimal side effects\n- Once-daily dosing is easy to maintain\n- Benefits are noticeable within a few weeks\n- It teaches you the fundamentals of peptide use\n\nWhat BPC-157 does:\n- Accelerates healing of tendons, ligaments, and muscles\n- Supports gut health and digestion\n- Reduces inflammation\n- Promotes tissue repair throughout the body\n\nYour 6-week journey:\nOver the next 42 days, you''ll learn:\n- How to reconstitute peptides\n- Proper injection technique\n- How to build a sustainable daily routine\n- What to expect as healing progresses\n\nThis is the perfect starting point. Master this, and you''ll be ready for more advanced protocols if you choose.", "action_item": "Read through My Plan tab. Familiarize yourself with BPC-157 and your simple daily schedule."},
    {"day": 1, "phase": "Getting Started", "title": "Understanding BPC-157", "content": "Today we go deep on the one peptide you''ll be using: BPC-157.\n\nWhat is BPC-157?\nBPC stands for \"Body Protection Compound.\" It''s a synthetic peptide derived from a protein naturally found in your stomach''s gastric juice. Your body already produces something similar—we''re just giving it a concentrated, targeted boost.\n\nHow it works:\nBPC-157 accelerates your body''s natural healing processes:\n\nTendons & Ligaments - Often the slowest to heal naturally, BPC-157 increases blood flow and collagen production to these tissues.\n\nMuscles - Speeds recovery from workouts and helps repair strains or tears faster.\n\nGut - Originally studied for digestive healing. Helps with leaky gut, IBS, and general digestive issues.\n\nSystemic Effects - Reduces inflammation throughout the body, supports nitric oxide production, and has even shown antidepressant-like effects in studies.\n\nThe research:\nBPC-157 has been extensively studied in animal models, showing remarkable healing effects. Human trials are limited, but thousands of users report results that align with the research.\n\nYour dosing:\n250mcg once daily for 6 weeks. That''s it. Simple.\n\nWhy once daily works:\nWhile some protocols use twice-daily dosing, once daily is effective for most goals and much easier to maintain, especially when you''re learning.", "action_item": "Consider your goals. Are you targeting a specific injury, or using this for general wellness and recovery?"},
    {"day": 2, "phase": "Getting Started", "title": "Your Supplies Checklist", "content": "Before your first injection, let''s make sure you have everything you need.\n\nRequired supplies:\n\nBPC-157:\n- 2 x 5mg vials (enough for the full 6-week course)\n- At 250mcg daily for 42 days, you''ll use about 10.5mg total\n- Two 5mg vials give you exactly what you need\n\nBacteriostatic Water:\n- 1 x 30ml vial\n- This is the sterile water used to reconstitute your peptides\n- Never use regular water—only bacteriostatic water\n\nInsulin Syringes:\n- 45 syringes (one per day plus spares)\n- 1ml syringes, 29-31 gauge\n- These are the thin, short needles designed for subcutaneous injection\n\nAlcohol Swabs:\n- 1 box of 100\n- For cleaning vial tops and injection sites\n\nSharps Container:\n- For safe disposal of used syringes\n- Never throw syringes in regular trash\n\nThe math:\nWhen you add 2ml of bacteriostatic water to a 5mg vial:\n- 0.1ml (10 units on your syringe) = 250mcg\n- Each vial = 20 doses\n- Two vials = 40 doses (plus a little extra)\n\nWe''ll cover this in detail on Reconstitution Day.", "action_item": "Verify you have all supplies. If anything is missing, order it now—tomorrow is reconstitution day."},
    {"day": 3, "phase": "Getting Started", "title": "Reconstitution Day", "content": "Today you''ll mix your first vial of BPC-157. This is easier than it sounds.\n\nWhat is reconstitution?\nPeptides come as a freeze-dried powder. You add bacteriostatic water to turn it into an injectable solution. This process is called reconstitution.\n\nStep-by-step:\n\n1. Gather supplies: BPC-157 vial, bacteriostatic water, syringe, alcohol swabs\n\n2. Let both vials warm to room temperature (about 5 minutes)\n\n3. Clean the rubber tops of both vials with alcohol swabs\n\n4. Draw 2ml of bacteriostatic water into your syringe (that''s 200 units, or a full 1ml syringe twice, or use a larger syringe)\n\n5. Insert the needle into the BPC-157 vial at an angle, with the tip against the glass wall\n\n6. SLOWLY release the water down the side of the vial (take 30-60 seconds)\n\n7. Do NOT shake the vial. Gently swirl if needed.\n\n8. Wait 5-10 minutes for complete dissolution\n\n9. Check: The solution should be completely clear with no particles\n\n10. Label the vial with today''s date and refrigerate\n\nStorage:\n- Keep reconstituted BPC-157 in the refrigerator\n- Use within 28 days\n- Never freeze\n\nCommon questions:\n- What if there are bubbles? Small bubbles are normal and will dissipate. Don''t shake trying to remove them.\n- What if it''s cloudy? Wait longer. If still cloudy after 15 minutes, something may be wrong with the peptide.", "action_item": "Reconstitute your first BPC-157 vial. Tomorrow you do your first injection."},
    {"day": 4, "phase": "Getting Started", "title": "Your First Injection", "content": "This is it—your first peptide injection. Take a breath. You''ve got this.\n\nStep-by-step injection guide:\n\n1. Wash your hands thoroughly\n\n2. Get your supplies: Reconstituted BPC-157, fresh syringe, alcohol swabs\n\n3. Clean the vial top with an alcohol swab\n\n4. Draw your dose:\n   - Pull the plunger to 10 units (0.1ml)\n   - Insert needle into vial, push air in\n   - Turn vial upside down\n   - Pull plunger to draw 10 units of solution\n   - Check for air bubbles—tap syringe to move them up, push them out\n\n5. Choose your injection site:\n   - If targeting a specific injury: inject within 2-3 inches of that area\n   - For general use: lower abdomen (2 inches from belly button)\n\n6. Clean the injection site with an alcohol swab, let it dry\n\n7. Pinch the skin to create a fold\n\n8. Insert the needle at a 45-degree angle\n\n9. Inject slowly (2-3 seconds)\n\n10. Wait 5 seconds, then remove the needle\n\n11. Don''t rub the site—gentle pressure is fine\n\n12. Dispose of the syringe in your sharps container\n\nHow it should feel:\nThe needle is thin—most people barely feel it. You might feel slight pressure during injection. That''s normal.\n\nCongratulations. You just did your first peptide injection.", "action_item": "Complete your first injection. Notice how it feels. You''ll get more comfortable quickly."},
    {"day": 5, "phase": "Getting Started", "title": "The Day After", "content": "How do you feel? Let''s check in.\n\nWhat''s normal:\n- No noticeable effects yet (very common)\n- Slight redness or small bump at injection site (normal, fades quickly)\n- Maybe a bit of excitement that you actually did it\n\nWhat''s not normal:\n- Significant pain or swelling (rare—contact healthcare provider)\n- Fever or flu-like symptoms (very rare—stop and consult)\n- Allergic reaction signs (extremely rare)\n\nMost people feel... nothing. That''s fine.\n\nBPC-157 works subtly:\n- You won''t feel a \"rush\" or immediate effect\n- Benefits build over days and weeks\n- Think of it like taking vitamins—no instant feedback, but cumulative benefit\n\nBuilding the habit:\nThe key to success is consistency. One injection, same time, every day. Here''s how to make it stick:\n\n- Tie it to an existing habit (morning coffee, brushing teeth, before bed)\n- Keep supplies in one place\n- Set a phone reminder if needed\n- It takes about 2 weeks for any habit to feel automatic\n\nYou''re on Day 2 of injections. 40 more to go. Each one gets easier.", "action_item": "Complete today''s injection. Start building the daily habit."},
    {"day": 6, "phase": "Getting Started", "title": "Finding Your Routine", "content": "Let''s lock in your daily routine.\n\nMorning or evening?\n\nMorning pros:\n- Get it done early, don''t think about it\n- Start the day knowing you''ve done something good for yourself\n- Easy to tie to morning routines\n\nEvening pros:\n- Some prefer injecting after training\n- Healing happens during sleep\n- Relaxed, no morning rush\n\nBoth work equally well. Choose based on what fits your life.\n\nMy recommended routine:\n\n1. Pick your time (same time every day)\n2. Pick your location (bathroom works well—private, near supplies)\n3. Create a trigger (after brushing teeth, before coffee, etc.)\n4. Do the injection (takes 2 minutes once you''re practiced)\n5. Check it off mentally or in an app\n\nRotating injection sites:\nTo avoid irritation, don''t inject in the exact same spot daily:\n- Lower left abdomen\n- Lower right abdomen\n- Around an injury site (different positions)\n- Thighs (if you prefer)\n\nThis is the simplest protocol there is. One injection. One time per day. Build the habit.", "action_item": "Decide: morning or evening? Commit to one and stick with it."},
    {"day": 7, "phase": "Getting Started", "title": "Week 1 Complete", "content": "You did it—one full week of peptide use.\n\nWhat you''ve accomplished:\n- Reconstituted your first vial\n- Completed 4 daily injections\n- Built the beginning of a consistent routine\n- Overcome any initial nervousness\n\nReflect on the week:\n- Are injections getting easier?\n- Have you found your preferred time and location?\n- Any issues with supplies or technique?\n\nWhat to expect from here:\nWeeks 2-3 are when most people start noticing subtle changes:\n- Reduced soreness after workouts\n- Mild improvement in targeted issues\n- Maybe better gut health\n- General sense of recovery improvement\n\nBe patient:\nBPC-157 isn''t instant. It supports your body''s natural healing processes, which take time. The consistency you''re building now is what creates results.\n\nYour first vial status:\n- 4 injections done = 4 doses used\n- 16 doses remaining in first vial\n- You''ll need to reconstitute your second vial around Day 20\n\nOne week down. Five to go. You''re doing great.", "action_item": "Complete today''s injection. Celebrate finishing your first week."},
    {"day": 8, "phase": "Building Consistency", "title": "Week 2 Begins", "content": "Welcome to Week 2. The foundation is set—now we build consistency.\n\nWeek 2 focus:\n- Maintain the daily habit without thinking about it\n- Start noticing subtle changes\n- Refine your technique\n\nWhat you might notice this week:\n- Injection feels more natural\n- Routine is becoming automatic\n- Possibly reduced post-workout soreness\n- Subtle improvements in targeted areas\n\nOr you might notice... nothing yet. That''s okay.\n\nTrust the process:\n- Healing takes time\n- BPC-157 supports healing, it doesn''t force it\n- Weeks 3-4 is when most people really notice\n\nYour job this week:\n- Show up every day\n- Do your injection\n- Track any changes (even subtle ones)\n\nConsistency is the entire game. Anyone can do one injection. Doing 42 is what creates results.", "action_item": "Complete today''s injection. Notice if the routine is feeling more natural."},
    {"day": 9, "phase": "Building Consistency", "title": "Tracking Progress", "content": "How do you know if BPC-157 is working? Let''s set up some tracking.\n\nWhat to track:\n\nIf targeting an injury:\n- Pain level (1-10 scale)\n- Range of motion\n- Functionality (what can you do now vs. before?)\n\nFor general recovery:\n- Post-workout soreness (1-10)\n- Recovery time between sessions\n- Energy levels\n\nFor gut health:\n- Digestive comfort\n- Bloating or discomfort frequency\n- Overall gut feeling\n\nSimple tracking method:\n- Rate key metrics 1-10 weekly\n- Note any noticeable changes\n- Keep it simple—don''t overcomplicate\n\nBaseline comparison:\nThink back to before you started:\n- How did your injury/issue feel on Day 0?\n- What was your recovery like before?\n- What brings you here in the first place?\n\nThis gives you something to compare against. Subtle improvements are easy to miss without a baseline.", "action_item": "Rate your key metrics today. You''ll compare again at Week 3 and Week 6."},
    {"day": 10, "phase": "Building Consistency", "title": "Injection Technique Tips", "content": "Let''s refine your injection technique now that you have some experience.\n\nCommon beginner issues:\n\nAir bubbles in syringe:\n- Tap the syringe to move bubbles to the top\n- Push plunger slightly to expel bubbles\n- Small bubbles in a subQ injection are harmless, but try to minimize them\n\nHesitation before injecting:\n- Normal at first, but slows you down\n- Quick, confident insertion is easier than slow\n- The needle is thin—it''s not painful\n\nInjection site irritation:\n- Rotate sites regularly\n- Make sure alcohol dries before injecting\n- Don''t inject in the same exact spot twice in a row\n\nDrawing the right amount:\n- 10 units = 0.1ml = your dose\n- Double-check before injecting\n- Better to slightly under-draw than over\n\nPro tips:\n- Pinch the skin firmly—creates a good target\n- 45-degree angle is ideal for subQ\n- Inject slowly, remove slowly\n- No need to aspirate (pull back) for subQ injections\n\nThe more you practice, the faster and easier this becomes. Many people can do the entire process in under 2 minutes.", "action_item": "Focus on smooth, confident technique today. Notice where you can improve."},
    {"day": 11, "phase": "Building Consistency", "title": "What If You Miss a Dose?", "content": "Life happens. What if you miss an injection?\n\nThe short answer:\nDon''t panic. One missed dose won''t ruin your progress.\n\nWhat to do:\n\nIf you remember later the same day:\n- Do the injection then\n- Resume normal schedule tomorrow\n\nIf you don''t remember until the next day:\n- Just do your normal injection\n- Don''t double up to \"catch up\"\n- Continue your regular schedule\n\nIf you miss multiple days:\n- Resume your schedule when you remember\n- Don''t try to make up missed doses\n- Just keep going\n\nWhy it''s not a big deal:\n- BPC-157 builds up cumulatively\n- One missed dose doesn''t reset progress\n- Consistency over time matters, not perfection\n\nPrevention:\n- Set a phone reminder\n- Tie to an unmissable habit\n- Keep supplies visible\n- Have your routine locked in\n\nThat said: try not to miss doses. Consistency is what produces results. But don''t stress if it happens once.", "action_item": "Set a backup reminder on your phone if you haven''t already."},
    {"day": 12, "phase": "Building Consistency", "title": "The Healing Timeline", "content": "Understanding how healing works helps set realistic expectations.\n\nHealing isn''t linear:\n- Some days feel great\n- Some days feel like no progress\n- This is completely normal\n\nHow BPC-157 supports healing:\n- Increases blood flow to damaged tissue\n- Promotes collagen production\n- Reduces inflammation\n- Supports cellular repair processes\n\nBut healing still takes time. BPC-157 accelerates—it doesn''t instant-fix.\n\nTypical timeline:\n\nWeek 1-2:\n- Body adjusting to peptide\n- Subtle improvements may begin\n- Building foundation\n\nWeek 3-4:\n- More noticeable changes\n- Recovery improvement clearer\n- Injuries starting to feel better\n\nWeek 5-6:\n- Significant progress\n- Clear difference from baseline\n- Results you can point to\n\nFactors affecting your timeline:\n- Severity of issue\n- How long you''ve had it (chronic takes longer)\n- Your overall health and nutrition\n- Sleep quality\n- Whether you''re reinjuring the area\n\nPatience is part of the protocol.", "action_item": "Trust the timeline. Keep showing up. The results are building."},
    {"day": 13, "phase": "Building Consistency", "title": "Supporting Your Healing", "content": "BPC-157 works with your body''s natural processes. Let''s support those processes.\n\nSleep (critical):\n- Most healing happens during sleep\n- Aim for 7-9 hours\n- Quality matters as much as quantity\n- This is when your body does its repair work\n\nProtein:\n- Your body needs amino acids to rebuild tissue\n- Aim for 0.7-1g per pound of bodyweight daily\n- Spread throughout the day\n- Complete proteins are best\n\nHydration:\n- Tissue repair requires adequate hydration\n- Minimum 8 glasses of water daily\n- More if you''re active\n\nAnti-inflammatory foods:\n- Colorful vegetables\n- Fatty fish (omega-3s)\n- Berries\n- Leafy greens\n- Reduce processed foods\n\nWhat to avoid:\n- Excessive alcohol (impairs healing)\n- NSAIDs if possible (ibuprofen, naproxen can interfere with healing)\n- Chronic stress (keeps inflammation high)\n- Reinjuring the area\n\nThe peptide amplifies what your body naturally does. Give your body what it needs to heal.", "action_item": "Identify one thing you can improve: sleep, protein, hydration, or stress management."},
    {"day": 14, "phase": "Building Consistency", "title": "Week 2 Complete", "content": "Two weeks done. You''re one-third of the way through.\n\nCheck in with yourself:\n\nRoutine:\n- Is daily injection automatic yet?\n- Any issues with supplies or technique?\n- Consistent with timing?\n\nPhysical:\n- Any changes noticed?\n- Recovery feeling different?\n- Injury improvement (if applicable)?\n\nMental:\n- Still motivated?\n- Comfortable with the process?\n- Any concerns?\n\nTypical Week 2 status:\n- Routine feels more natural\n- Maybe some subtle improvements\n- Still early for major changes\n- Building consistency\n\nYour first vial:\n- 11 injections done\n- ~9 doses remaining\n- Will need second vial around Day 20-21\n\nWeek 3 is often when people start noticing more tangible changes. Keep going.", "action_item": "Complete today''s injection. Celebrate two weeks of consistency."},
    {"day": 15, "phase": "Building Consistency", "title": "Week 3 - Changes Begin", "content": "Week 3. This is often when subtle becomes noticeable.\n\nWhat people typically notice around now:\n\nRecovery:\n- Less soreness after workouts\n- Bounce back faster between sessions\n- Can train with more intensity\n\nInjuries:\n- Noticeable improvement in pain levels\n- Better range of motion\n- More functionality\n\nGeneral:\n- More energy\n- Better digestion (if that was an issue)\n- Overall sense of feeling good\n\nOr you might notice... still nothing dramatic. That''s okay too.\n\nEveryone responds differently:\n- Some people are fast responders\n- Some need more time\n- Chronic issues take longer than acute\n- Keep going regardless\n\nRemember your baseline:\n- Compare to Day 0, not to yesterday\n- Subtle daily changes add up\n- Progress isn''t always linear\n\nYou''re doing the work. The results are coming.", "action_item": "Rate your key metrics and compare to your Day 9 ratings. Notice any improvement."},
    {"day": 16, "phase": "Building Consistency", "title": "Training with BPC-157", "content": "How does BPC-157 affect your training? Let''s discuss.\n\nImproved recovery means:\n- You can train more frequently\n- You can train harder\n- You can handle more volume\n- You bounce back faster\n\nBut don''t go crazy:\n- The peptide supports recovery—it doesn''t make you invincible\n- Progressive overload still applies\n- Injury prevention is still important\n- Listen to your body\n\nSmart adjustments:\n\nIf you''re recovering faster:\n- Add a set or two per workout\n- Slightly increase weights\n- Consider an extra session if time allows\n\nIf targeting an injury:\n- Gradually reintroduce load\n- Use pain as your guide (no sharp pain)\n- Don''t rush back to full intensity\n- Rehab work is still important\n\nInjection timing around training:\n- Before or after—doesn''t matter much\n- Some like injecting near worked muscles post-workout\n- Abdomen injection works regardless of training\n- Consistency matters more than perfect timing\n\nEnjoy the improved recovery. Use it wisely.", "action_item": "If recovery feels better, consider a small training adjustment this week."},
    {"day": 17, "phase": "Building Consistency", "title": "Troubleshooting Common Issues", "content": "Let''s address common concerns that come up around this point.\n\nNot feeling anything:\n- This is the most common \"problem\"\n- BPC-157 works subtly\n- Compare to your baseline, not to expectations\n- Give it through Week 4 minimum\n- Some people don''t notice until they stop\n\nInjection site issues:\n- Small red spot: Normal, fades quickly\n- Bruising: You may have hit a small vessel, rotate sites more\n- Irritation: Make sure alcohol dries before injecting\n- Hard lump: Rotate sites, it will absorb\n\nRoutine challenges:\n- Forgetting doses: Set reminders, tie to habits\n- Running low on supplies: Check your vial levels, plan ahead\n- Getting bored: Remember why you started, results are building\n\nUnexpected tiredness:\n- Rare with BPC-157 but can happen\n- Usually temporary\n- Make sure sleep and nutrition are solid\n- If persistent, consult healthcare provider\n\nMost issues are minor and resolve on their own. The key is to keep going.", "action_item": "Address any issues you''re experiencing. Don''t let small problems derail progress."},
    {"day": 18, "phase": "Building Consistency", "title": "The Power of Consistency", "content": "You''re 18 days in. Let''s talk about why consistency is everything.\n\nThe math:\n- 18 injections completed\n- ~4.5mg of BPC-157 in your system\n- Therapeutic levels building\n- Cumulative effect growing\n\nWhy daily matters:\nBPC-157 has a short half-life (about 4 hours). Daily dosing maintains consistent levels in your body. Miss too many doses and levels drop.\n\nBut it''s forgiving:\n- One missed dose doesn''t reset everything\n- The cumulative effect persists\n- Just get back on track\n\nThe habit is the goal:\nRight now, you''re not just taking a peptide—you''re building a skill. The ability to:\n- Commit to something daily\n- Follow through consistently\n- Trust a process you can''t see working\n\nThis skill transfers:\n- To more advanced peptide protocols\n- To other health habits\n- To anything requiring consistent effort\n\nYou''re 42% through. Keep building.", "action_item": "Acknowledge the consistency you''ve built. It''s an accomplishment."},
    {"day": 19, "phase": "Building Consistency", "title": "Preparing Your Second Vial", "content": "Your first vial is getting low. Time to prepare.\n\nCurrent status:\n- 16 injections done from first vial\n- ~4 doses remaining\n- Second vial needs reconstitution soon\n\nWhen to reconstitute:\n- Do it when you have 2-3 doses left in current vial\n- Having backup ready prevents gaps\n- Tomorrow or Day 20 is ideal timing\n\nReconstitution reminder:\n1. Clean new vial top with alcohol\n2. Add 2ml bacteriostatic water slowly\n3. Don''t shake—swirl gently\n4. Wait for complete dissolution (clear solution)\n5. Label with date\n6. Refrigerate\n\nStoring the new vial:\n- Both vials can be in the fridge\n- Finish the first vial completely before starting the second\n- Use within 28 days of reconstitution\n\nSupply check:\n- Enough syringes for remaining 23 days?\n- Alcohol swabs adequate?\n- Sharps container not full?\n\nPlanning ahead prevents problems.", "action_item": "Check your first vial level. Reconstitute your second vial if you have 3 or fewer doses left."},
    {"day": 20, "phase": "Building Consistency", "title": "Reconstitute Second Vial", "content": "If you haven''t already, today is the day to prepare your second vial.\n\nYou know the process now:\n1. Gather supplies\n2. Let new vial reach room temperature\n3. Clean the top with alcohol\n4. Add 2ml bacteriostatic water (slowly, down the side)\n5. Swirl gently—don''t shake\n6. Wait until completely clear\n7. Label with today''s date\n8. Refrigerate\n\nSwitching vials:\n- Finish your first vial completely\n- Then switch to the new one\n- No gap in dosing needed\n\nYou''re a pro at this now:\n- Remember your first reconstitution on Day 3?\n- How nervous you might have been?\n- Now it''s routine\n\nThis is the transformation:\n- From \"I don''t know if I can do this\"\n- To \"This is just what I do\"\n\nTwo-thirds of the course remains. You''ve got this.", "action_item": "Reconstitute your second BPC-157 vial. Complete today''s injection."},
    {"day": 21, "phase": "Building Consistency", "title": "Week 3 Complete - Halfway Point", "content": "Three weeks done. You''re officially at the halfway point.\n\nMajor milestone:\n- 21 injections completed\n- 21 more to go\n- Halfway through the course\n\nProgress check:\n\nCompare to Week 1:\n- How does the injection routine feel now?\n- Any noticeable physical changes?\n- Overall satisfaction with the process?\n\nTypical Week 3 observations:\n- Routine is fully automatic\n- Some improvements noticeable\n- Confidence in the process\n- Starting to see why people use peptides\n\nThe second half:\nWeeks 4-6 are often where results become undeniable:\n- Continued improvement\n- Clearer comparison to baseline\n- Satisfying payoff for consistency\n\nWhat comes after:\nStart thinking about Week 6:\n- Will you stop and assess?\n- Consider a more advanced protocol?\n- Take a break and potentially restart?\n\nNo decisions needed now. Just awareness.", "action_item": "Celebrate the halfway point. Note your progress compared to Day 0."},
    {"day": 22, "phase": "Seeing Results", "title": "Phase 3 - Seeing Results", "content": "Welcome to Phase 3. The next two weeks are typically when results become clear.\n\nWhat to expect:\n\nRecovery (most common benefit):\n- Noticeably less sore after training\n- Can handle more volume or frequency\n- Feel ready to go again sooner\n\nInjuries (if applicable):\n- Significant improvement from baseline\n- Better function and less pain\n- Closer to normal than before\n\nGut health (if that was a goal):\n- Improved digestion\n- Less bloating or discomfort\n- Better overall gut feeling\n\nGeneral well-being:\n- More energy\n- Better recovery from life, not just training\n- Overall sense of improved health\n\nIf you''re not there yet:\n- Chronic issues take longer\n- Everyone responds at their own pace\n- Benefits may become clear only after you stop\n- Keep going—you''re invested\n\nThe final two weeks are about maximizing and documenting results.", "action_item": "Rate your key metrics. Compare to Day 9 and Day 15. Document improvements."},
    {"day": 23, "phase": "Seeing Results", "title": "Maximizing Final Weeks", "content": "Let''s make these last three weeks count.\n\nMaximizing results:\n\nStay consistent:\n- Don''t get lazy now\n- Every injection matters\n- Finish what you started\n\nSupport the process:\n- Sleep: 7-9 hours, prioritize quality\n- Protein: Adequate intake daily\n- Hydration: Stay well-hydrated\n- Stress: Manage what you can\n\nTraining smart:\n- Take advantage of improved recovery\n- Push appropriately, don''t overdo it\n- Avoid reinjury if recovering from something\n\nDocument everything:\n- How do you feel?\n- What''s improved?\n- What metrics have changed?\n- This info is valuable for future decisions\n\nThe work you did in Weeks 1-3 is paying off now. These final weeks are where you see the return.", "action_item": "Identify one way to optimize your remaining weeks: sleep, nutrition, or training."},
    {"day": 24, "phase": "Seeing Results", "title": "Learning from This Experience", "content": "You''re learning more than just how to inject peptides.\n\nSkills you''ve developed:\n\nPractical:\n- How to reconstitute peptides\n- Proper injection technique\n- Supply management\n- Building a consistent routine\n\nMental:\n- Patience with a process\n- Trust in something you can''t immediately feel\n- Commitment over weeks, not just days\n- Self-discipline in health practices\n\nKnowledge:\n- How BPC-157 works\n- How healing happens\n- What supports recovery\n- Realistic expectations for peptides\n\nWhat this enables:\n- More advanced peptide protocols if interested\n- Better understanding of your body\n- Foundation for other health optimizations\n- Ability to evaluate claims about peptides\n\nYou started as a complete beginner. You''ll finish with real skills and knowledge.", "action_item": "Reflect on what you''ve learned. You''ve come a long way from Day 0."},
    {"day": 25, "phase": "Seeing Results", "title": "Considering What''s Next", "content": "Start thinking about life after Day 42.\n\nYour options:\n\nOption 1: Stop and assess\n- Complete the course, then stop\n- See how you feel without the peptide\n- Benefits often persist for weeks\n- Helps you understand what BPC-157 was actually doing\n- Can restart anytime\n\nOption 2: Take a break, then continue\n- 4-week break after this course\n- Run another 6-week course\n- Common approach for chronic issues\n- Prevents potential tolerance\n\nOption 3: Graduate to advanced protocols\n- Add TB-500 (the Muscle & Performance stack)\n- Try different peptides for different goals\n- Build on your foundation\n\nOption 4: Continue indefinitely (lower priority)\n- Ongoing low-dose maintenance\n- Discuss with healthcare provider for long-term use\n\nNo wrong answer:\n- Depends on your goals\n- Depends on your results\n- Depends on your situation\n\nYou don''t need to decide yet. Just start thinking.", "action_item": "Consider your options for after Day 42. What sounds right for you?"},
    {"day": 26, "phase": "Seeing Results", "title": "Week 4 Check-In", "content": "Four weeks done. Let''s take stock.\n\nProgress review:\n\nInjection routine:\n- Completely automatic by now?\n- Any remaining challenges?\n- Technique refined?\n\nPhysical results:\n- Recovery improvement (rate 1-10 vs. baseline)\n- Injury progress (if applicable, rate 1-10 vs. baseline)\n- Energy levels\n- Any other noticeable changes\n\nMental state:\n- Satisfied with the experience?\n- Glad you started?\n- Clear on what comes next?\n\nTypical Week 4 status:\n- Clear improvement from baseline\n- Routine is effortless\n- Confidence in the process\n- Starting to think about next steps\n\nTwo weeks left:\n- Continue the routine\n- Maximize remaining time\n- Document final results\n- Prepare for course completion", "action_item": "Full progress review. Compare all metrics to Day 0."},
    {"day": 27, "phase": "Seeing Results", "title": "Staying Motivated", "content": "11 days left. Let''s finish strong.\n\nCommon Week 5 feelings:\n\n\"It''s working, I''m happy\":\n- Great! Keep going\n- Document your results\n- Consider your next steps\n\n\"It''s helping, but I expected more\":\n- Chronic issues take longer\n- Consider another course\n- Results vary by individual\n- Some benefits only clear after stopping\n\n\"I''m not sure if it''s doing anything\":\n- Track objective measures (training, pain scales)\n- Many people notice when they stop\n- Finish the course before judging\n- Subtle improvements are still improvements\n\nRegardless of how you feel:\n- Finish the 42 days\n- You''ve invested this much\n- Complete data is more valuable than partial\n- 11 more injections isn''t much\n\nThe end is in sight. Push through.", "action_item": "Recommit to finishing strong. 11 more days of consistency."},
    {"day": 28, "phase": "Seeing Results", "title": "Week 4 Complete", "content": "Four weeks done. Two weeks remaining.\n\nYou''ve accomplished:\n- 28 daily injections\n- 7mg of BPC-157 administered\n- A solid 4-week peptide protocol\n- Real experience and knowledge\n\nWhat you''ve learned:\n- How to reconstitute and inject\n- How to build a daily health habit\n- How healing works over time\n- What peptides can (and can''t) do\n\nThe final stretch:\n- 14 more injections\n- 2 more weeks\n- Then assessment time\n\nWeeks 5-6:\n- Maintain the routine\n- Continue documenting\n- Prepare for course completion\n- Make decisions about next steps\n\nYou''re a peptide user now, not a beginner wondering if you can do this.", "action_item": "Complete today''s injection. Two weeks to go."},
    {"day": 29, "phase": "Seeing Results", "title": "Final Stretch Begins", "content": "13 days remaining. The home stretch.\n\nFinal weeks focus:\n\nMaintain consistency:\n- Same routine, same time\n- Don''t get lazy now\n- Every injection counts\n\nDocument results:\n- What''s improved?\n- By how much?\n- What surprised you?\n\nPlan ahead:\n- What happens on Day 43?\n- Supplies for potential next course?\n- Healthcare provider consultation if needed?\n\nThe finish line effect:\n- It''s tempting to coast\n- Don''t—finish strong\n- These final doses still contribute\n\nYou''ve done 28 days. 14 more is easy.", "action_item": "Continue your routine. Start final documentation."},
    {"day": 30, "phase": "Seeing Results", "title": "Reflecting on the Journey", "content": "Day 30. Time for reflection.\n\nThink back to Day 0:\n- How did you feel about peptides?\n- What were your concerns?\n- What were you hoping for?\n\nNow:\n- How do you feel about peptides?\n- Were your concerns valid?\n- Did you get what you hoped for?\n\nFor most people:\n\nBefore: \"Can I really do this? Will it work?\"\nAfter: \"This isn''t hard. I see why people use peptides.\"\n\nBefore: \"Injections seem scary.\"\nAfter: \"It takes 2 minutes and I barely feel it.\"\n\nBefore: \"I hope this helps with [X].\"\nAfter: \"I''ve definitely noticed improvement in [X].\"\n\nYour specific experience may vary, but the transformation from beginner to experienced is real.", "action_item": "Reflect on your journey from Day 0 to now. Appreciate the growth."},
    {"day": 31, "phase": "Seeing Results", "title": "Continuing the Routine", "content": "11 days remaining. Keep the routine going.\n\nDaily checklist:\n☐ BPC-157 injection (your chosen time)\n☐ Adequate sleep\n☐ Enough protein\n☐ Staying hydrated\n☐ Training smart\n\nThis is autopilot now:\n- You don''t need to think about it\n- Just execute\n- The habits are locked in\n\nWhat you might be feeling:\n- Satisfaction with consistency\n- Clear results vs. baseline\n- Maybe ready to be done\n- Possibly interested in more\n\nAll valid. Finish the course, then decide.", "action_item": "Complete today''s injection. Stay on autopilot."},
    {"day": 32, "phase": "Seeing Results", "title": "Supply Check", "content": "10 days left. Make sure supplies last.\n\nVial status:\n- How many doses in your current vial?\n- Should be enough to finish\n- If low, you reconstituted at the right time\n\nOther supplies:\n- Syringes: Need 10 more\n- Alcohol swabs: Adequate?\n- Sharps container: Not overflowing?\n\nPlanning for after:\n- If stopping: No new supplies needed\n- If continuing: Order for next course now\n- If unsure: Wait and decide\n\nNo emergencies at the finish line:\n- Running out of supplies with days left is frustrating\n- A quick check now prevents issues\n- Better to have extra than not enough\n\nAlmost there.", "action_item": "Verify you have supplies for 10 more days. No gaps at the finish."},
    {"day": 33, "phase": "Seeing Results", "title": "Week 5 Progress", "content": "Week 5 in progress. Everything should be smooth by now.\n\nTypical Week 5 status:\n\nPhysically:\n- Clear improvement from baseline\n- Recovery noticeably better\n- Any targeted issues improved\n- Benefits feel \"normal\" now\n\nMentally:\n- Confident in the process\n- Routine is effortless\n- Ready to finish\n- Thinking about next steps\n\nThe \"new normal\":\n- You might forget how you felt before\n- Review your Day 0 notes\n- The contrast is often surprising\n- Easy to take improvement for granted\n\nOne more week after this:\n- Then you''re done\n- A full 6-week course completed\n- Ready for whatever comes next", "action_item": "Compare current state to Day 0 notes. Notice how far you''ve come."},
    {"day": 34, "phase": "Seeing Results", "title": "What You''ll Take With You", "content": "Regardless of whether you continue peptides, you''ll keep:\n\nSkills:\n- How to reconstitute peptides\n- Proper injection technique\n- Supply management\n- Building consistent habits\n\nKnowledge:\n- How peptides work\n- Realistic expectations\n- How to evaluate peptide claims\n- Understanding of healing processes\n\nHealing:\n- Tissue repair that occurred is permanent\n- Injuries that improved stay improved\n- Recovery gains can be maintained with good habits\n\nConfidence:\n- You can commit to a 6-week protocol\n- You can inject daily without issue\n- You can see something through\n- You can try more advanced protocols\n\nThis foundation is valuable whether or not you use peptides again.", "action_item": "Acknowledge what you''re taking with you beyond the physical benefits."},
    {"day": 35, "phase": "Seeing Results", "title": "Week 5 Complete", "content": "Five weeks done. One week remaining.\n\nFinal week preview:\n- 7 more injections\n- Continue routine\n- Final documentation\n- Prepare for course end\n\nThis week:\n- Stay consistent to the end\n- Don''t coast\n- Make final notes\n- Decide on next steps\n\nAlmost there:\n- You''ve demonstrated consistency\n- You''ve seen what BPC-157 can do\n- You''ve completed a real peptide protocol\n- One more week\n\nFinish strong.", "action_item": "Complete today''s injection. One week to go."},
    {"day": 36, "phase": "Completion", "title": "Final Week Begins", "content": "Day 36. The final week.\n\n6 injections remaining:\n- Today\n- Days 37-41\n- Day 42 (final)\n\nThis week''s focus:\n\nMaintain routine:\n- Same time, same place\n- Don''t skip the final days\n- Finish what you started\n\nDocument results:\n- Final comparison to baseline\n- All improvements noted\n- Lessons learned\n- Decisions for future\n\nPrepare for after:\n- Know your plan\n- Supplies if continuing\n- Mental preparation for change in routine\n\nThe end is here. Make it count.", "action_item": "Complete today''s injection. 6 more days."},
    {"day": 37, "phase": "Completion", "title": "5 Days Remaining", "content": "Counting down now. 5 injections left.\n\nFinal results assessment:\n\nRate 1-10 compared to Day 0:\n- Overall recovery: __\n- Specific injury (if applicable): __\n- Energy levels: __\n- General well-being: __\n\nMost beginners see:\n- Recovery: 6-8 (moderate to significant improvement)\n- Injuries: 5-8 (depending on severity and chronicity)\n- Energy: 6-8 (moderate improvement)\n- Well-being: 7-8 (generally positive)\n\nYour numbers may vary:\n- Individual response differs\n- Chronic issues take longer\n- Some benefits subtle\n- Overall, most people are satisfied\n\nDocument for future reference.", "action_item": "Rate your final results. Compare to all previous check-ins."},
    {"day": 38, "phase": "Completion", "title": "4 Days Remaining", "content": "4 injections to go.\n\nFinal thoughts on the experience:\n\nWhat worked:\n- The simplicity (one peptide, once daily)\n- The gradual improvement\n- The skill development\n- The routine building\n\nWhat you might do differently:\n- Started sooner?\n- Better documentation from Day 1?\n- More focus on supporting factors?\n- Different expectations?\n\nFor future courses (if any):\n- You now know what to expect\n- Can set better baselines\n- More realistic timeline expectations\n- Better supporting habits\n\nLessons learned are valuable.", "action_item": "Note what you''d do differently. This helps future decisions."},
    {"day": 39, "phase": "Completion", "title": "3 Days Remaining", "content": "Almost there. 3 injections remaining.\n\nWhat happens when you stop:\n\nNo withdrawal:\n- BPC-157 is not habit-forming\n- No physical dependency\n- No need to taper\n- Just stop after Day 42\n\nWhat persists:\n- Healing that occurred is permanent\n- Skills you learned remain\n- Improved tissue doesn''t \"un-heal\"\n\nWhat fades:\n- Active recovery support\n- Ongoing anti-inflammatory effect\n- The \"boost\" gradually decreases\n\nTimeline after stopping:\n- Week 1: Feel about the same\n- Week 2: Still feeling benefits\n- Week 3-4: May notice recovery returning to baseline\n- After: You''ll know what the peptide was doing\n\nThis helps you decide about future courses.", "action_item": "Understand what to expect post-course. No surprises."},
    {"day": 40, "phase": "Completion", "title": "2 Days Remaining", "content": "Penultimate day. 2 injections left (today and tomorrow).\n\nFinal documentation:\n\nKey metrics (final rating vs. Day 0):\n- Recovery: Before __ → Now __\n- Pain/injury: Before __ → Now __\n- Energy: Before __ → Now __\n- Overall: Before __ → Now __\n\nQualitative notes:\n- Biggest improvement?\n- Any surprises?\n- Would you recommend to others?\n- Will you do another course?\n\nThis data:\n- Helps you remember the experience\n- Informs future decisions\n- Proves to yourself it worked (or didn''t)\n- Is something you can reference\n\nOne more day after today.", "action_item": "Complete final documentation. Injection tomorrow is your last."},
    {"day": 41, "phase": "Completion", "title": "Final Day", "content": "Day 41. Tomorrow is Day 42—your last injection.\n\nReflect on 6 weeks:\n\nYou did it:\n- 41 injections completed\n- 6 weeks of consistency\n- A complete peptide course\n- Real results achieved\n\nFrom beginner to experienced:\n- Day 0: Nervous, unsure, hoping it would work\n- Day 41: Confident, skilled, knowing what peptides can do\n\nThis is significant:\n- Most people don''t follow through\n- You committed and delivered\n- That discipline applies elsewhere\n- You should be proud\n\nTomorrow:\n- Final injection\n- Course complete\n- New chapter begins\n\nPrepare for the finish.", "action_item": "Complete today''s injection. Tomorrow is the finale."},
    {"day": 42, "phase": "Completion", "title": "Course Complete!", "content": "Day 42. You did it.\n\n6 weeks complete:\n- 42 daily injections\n- ~10.5mg of BPC-157 administered\n- A full beginner course finished\n- Real skills and results achieved\n\nWhat you''ve accomplished:\n\nPhysical:\n- Improved recovery\n- Healing of targeted issues\n- Better overall well-being\n- Enhanced training capacity\n\nSkills:\n- Peptide reconstitution\n- Injection technique\n- Consistent habit building\n- Self-discipline\n\nKnowledge:\n- How peptides work\n- Realistic expectations\n- Supporting factors for healing\n- Foundation for advanced protocols\n\nWhat''s next:\n\nOption 1: Stop and assess\n- See how you feel without peptides\n- Benefits often persist for weeks\n- Can restart anytime\n\nOption 2: Take a break, then continue\n- 4-week break\n- Another BPC-157 course\n- Or try adding TB-500 (Muscle course)\n\nOption 3: Explore other goals\n- Different peptides for different goals\n- Cognitive, anti-aging, fat loss options\n- You have the skills now\n\nThank you for completing the Beginner Course.\n\nYou''re no longer a beginner. You''re someone who has successfully completed a peptide protocol. Whatever you choose next, you have the foundation.\n\nCongratulations.", "action_item": "Complete your final injection. Celebrate. Decide your next move."}
